---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import * as stylex from "@stylexjs/stylex";
import { caseStudyStyles } from "../../styles/caseStudyStyles";
import { portfolioStyles } from "../../styles/portfolioStyles";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Dataset Query System | Warre Snaet"
  description="A Python client-server app for analyzing a shared dataset, with PySide6 desktop interfaces and PostgreSQL."
  kicker="Individual academic project, 2025"
  heading="Dataset query system"
  lead="Logged-in users query a dataset of Los Angeles arrests that stays on the server, from a desktop app that talks to it over TCP sockets. A separate server window lets the operator watch clients and send them messages."
  meta={[
    { label: "My role", value: "Designed and built the whole application" },
    { label: "Stack", value: "Python, TCP sockets, pickle, PySide6, PostgreSQL, pandas, matplotlib, folium" },
    { label: "Data", value: "Los Angeles arrests from 2020 on (Kaggle)" },
  ]}
>
  <CaseSection label="Problem" title="Querying data without handing out the dataset">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>The dataset and the analysis code live on the server, and clients ask it for specific statistics and charts. That meant the app needed a clear network protocol, a server that handles several clients at once, authentication, a moderator view, and desktop interfaces on both sides that stay responsive.</p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Architecture" title="Separate interface, protocol, and processing">
    <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.architecture)} aria-label="Dataset query client-server architecture">
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Client</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>PySide6 desktop application</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Login, parameterized queries, results, messages, and history</span></div>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowArrow)}>↕ TCP</div>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Server</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Threaded client handlers</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>One thread per connection. Messages are pickled Python objects with a length prefix, gzipped above 1 MB</span></div>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowArrow)}>↓</div>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Data layer</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>PostgreSQL and pandas</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>PostgreSQL holds users and query history. A pool of four workers runs the pandas queries with a timeout</span></div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Delivered" title="A client app and a server app">
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Built registration and login, and a separate PySide6 server window for the moderator, with tabs for server control, messages, clients, and statistics.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Built four parameterized queries: arrests by area and date with an age filter, how a charge type changes over time, arrests by descent and sex, and arrest hotspots within a radius drawn on a folium map.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Let the moderator see who is connected, each client's query history, which queries are popular, and the live log, and send a message to everyone or to one client.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Used worker threads and Qt signals so networking and large query results don't block the interface.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>Moved storage from SQLite to PostgreSQL in Docker, with a connection pool, query timeouts, and memory limits.</li>
    </ul>
    <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.links)}><a {...stylex.attrs(portfolioStyles.link, caseStudyStyles.importantLink)} href="https://github.com/SnaetWarre/Server-Client-Python">View repository ↗</a></div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Lesson" title="Concurrency is part of interface design">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>The query logic itself wasn't the hard constraint. Keeping network traffic, analysis work, and GUI updates apart was. A correct result doesn't help much if the window freezes while the data comes in or a plot is being generated.</p>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
